1. The Physics of 3D Depth (Multi-Tiered Lighting Model)
In real life, an object sitting on or hovering above a surface never has just one blurry shadow. It interacts with light through 4 distinct physical optical layers.

Here is how .depth-card was engineered:

/* Real Physical Lighting in CSS */
box-shadow: 
  /* 1. Specular Rim Light (Top edge catch-light) */
  0 1px 0 0 rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.16) inset,

  /* 2. Bevel Occlusion (Bottom inner self-shadow) */
  0 -1px 0 0 rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.6) inset,

  /* 3. Primary Ambient Drop (Soft floating elevation) */
  0 14px 28px -6px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.75),

  /* 4. Contact Occlusion (Dark, crisp shadow right underneath) */
  0 4px 10px -2px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.5);
Why it works:
The Top Rim (0 1px 0 inset): Simulates a studio ceiling light hitting the microscopic top chamfer of the glass/aluminum slab. This gives the card crisp edge definition even against dark backgrounds.

Ambient vs Contact Shadow: Splitting the drop shadow into two (one wide & diffuse 28px, one sharp & dark 4px) replicates real-world ambient occlusion.

2. The Color Science & Surface Elevation Hierarchy
Flat designs fail because they use a single gray/black background and place cards with identical colors on top.

In this system, we used a luminance stepping technique inspired by Apple's OLED dark mode guidelines:

Layer	Dark Mode Token	Light Mode Token	Physical Analogy
Base Canvas	#0A0A0C (True Obsidian)	#EBF0F5 (Soft Alabaster)	The desk or tabletop
Recessed Trenches	#0E0E12 (Sunken OLED)	#D9DFE8 (Etched Slate)	Carved grooves in metal
Elevated Slabs	linear-gradient(#22222B, #17171C)	linear-gradient(#FFFFFF, #F6F8FB)	Anodized dark titanium slate
Active Floating Pills	linear-gradient(#3A3A44, #282830)	linear-gradient(#FFFFFF, #E9ECEF)	Raised mechanical tactile button
Notice that gradients are used instead of flat solid fills. Subtle 165° directional angle gradients mimic how natural light falls obliquely across a phone screen.

3. "Carved Trenches" vs "Floating Slabs"
To create dramatic depth, you need contrast between convex (popping out) and concave (carved in) elements:

Segmented Controls & Input Fields (.carved-trench): Instead of an outline border, we applied dual inner shadows:

  box-shadow: 
    inset 0 2px 5px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.8),  /* Deep top inner shadow */
    inset 0 1px 2px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.9),  /* Lip shadow */
    0 1px 0 rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.08);   /* Bottom rim bounce highlight */
  
This tricks the human brain into seeing a physically stamped or milled recess into the phone chassis.

The Active Pill (.depth-pill): When a pill sits inside a carved trench, it pops outward because its gradient is brighter than the trench floor, and it casts its own subtle drop shadow onto the recessed floor.

